WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:09.700
Welcome, everyone. This is the NCBI Minute
on how to use EDirect's Xtract utility to

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:12.809
parse BLAST XML output.

00:00:12.809 --> 00:00:17.919
What I will do today is -- remind you about
the EDirect command line programs that Eric

00:00:17.919 --> 00:00:23.619
talked about -- almost 3 weeks ago. Then I
will show you a little about the BLAST xml

00:00:23.619 --> 00:00:27.489
output and tell you why you might want to
use that and then I will do a couple of quick

00:00:27.489 --> 00:00:35.480
examples using EDirect to reformat the xml
output into a human readable form and it might

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:41.420
be a good way to save your BLAST results for
whatever you want to do with them.

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:49.239
As mentioned previously, Eric gave a full
tutorial on using EDirect, the command line

00:00:49.239 --> 00:00:55.159
utility for accessing the E-utilities. It
is a way of using the E-utilities without

00:00:55.159 --> 00:00:59.399
having to write a script yourself. This is
a set of scripts that search and retrieve

00:00:59.399 --> 00:01:08.720
information from Entrez. What comes with it
is a utility called Xtract, a general xml

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:15.700
parser and we will show you how to use it
to parse another kind of NCBI XML, the XML

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:23.500
produced by BLAST. If you look at the command
line there that Eric has from his talk a few

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:30.110
weeks ago it is piped into Xtract at the end
to produce a report from this particular set

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:35.290
of commands they issued through the EDirect
scripts.

00:01:35.290 --> 00:01:40.730
This is looking at the standalone options
-- BLAST produces different structured outputs.

00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:47.210
Structured outputs, some in particular, have
more complete information than any of the

00:01:47.210 --> 00:01:52.570
other flatfile outputs. Most people are familiar
with the pairwise. It doesn't have everything

00:01:52.570 --> 00:02:00.690
in it. The other formats like ASN.1, XML,
Jason, have more information. The XML output

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:07.610
formats are probably the most convenient for
parsing because you can use EDirect to do

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:13.999
this. There are two different kinds -- the
traditional BLAST XML and the XML2 which is

00:02:13.999 --> 00:02:16.239
relatively new.

00:02:16.239 --> 00:02:21.319
You can also get these from a web service.
What I'm talking about today is perfectly

00:02:21.319 --> 00:02:26.099
general and in fact I will show you the example
that I'm using coming from doing a web search

00:02:26.099 --> 00:02:29.629
and I'll show you why you might want to do
this from a web search although it may not

00:02:29.629 --> 00:02:32.959
be immediately obvious why you would want
to do that.

00:02:32.959 --> 00:02:38.049
If you expand the download tab on the blast
page or the download section on the blast

00:02:38.049 --> 00:02:43.549
results page you can get a lot of different
formats. The classic BLAST XML comes down

00:02:43.549 --> 00:02:50.650
as a single XML document and the XML 2 is
a zip file because it has an individual document

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:55.400
for each of the queries and then there is
an Xinclude document to let you put those

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:59.599
back together if you need to. Sometimes it's
convenient to have them separate and sometimes

00:02:59.599 --> 00:03:04.349
it's convenient to have them as one document;
it depends on what you are trying to do.

00:03:04.349 --> 00:03:10.499
Here is a view of the XML, a little bit. The
classic BLAST XML we've had around for a long

00:03:10.499 --> 00:03:15.389
time, you get a single document to the whole
thing. We'll talk in a minute about when you

00:03:15.389 --> 00:03:20.029
are parsing this -- the thing that contains
the results in this xml is Iteration. For

00:03:20.029 --> 00:03:27.779
the BLAST XML 2 as I said you have a master
document and a document for each query. There's

00:03:27.779 --> 00:03:32.519
a link to more information about the XML 2
if you want to read more about it. If you

00:03:32.519 --> 00:03:38.999
need to combine them, as we'll do today, you
can use this utility called xmllint to pull

00:03:38.999 --> 00:03:41.060
it together for processing.

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:46.989
The XML2 has a number of advantages. One is
that it has full support for taxonomy so you

00:03:46.989 --> 00:03:52.169
can get the tax ID and the scientific name
out of that. It also supports iterative blast,

00:03:52.169 --> 00:03:58.099
like psi-blast and blast 2 sequences, which
are not supported properly in the old classic

00:03:58.099 --> 00:04:05.359
blast XML. And for parsing this, the container
that has things in it that we want is results.

00:04:05.359 --> 00:04:10.559
As you saw in Eric's talk -- you can go back
and review that if you want -- when we parse

00:04:10.559 --> 00:04:18.480
the XML using Xtract we are using nested for
loops and there is a hierarchy based on some

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:24.750
of the exploration commands that are part of EDirect.
We'll be using pattern as our highest level,

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:30.090
that's the highest level that you usually
use, and block to indicate a lower level -- the

00:04:30.090 --> 00:04:34.990
pattern, for example in the classic XML will
be iteration, and then we can look at the

00:04:34.990 --> 00:04:39.310
individual hits if we're trying to pull those
out of a block.

00:04:39.310 --> 00:04:46.550
Before we go on to the demo I want to point
out that there is a document -- chapter 6

00:04:46.550 --> 00:04:51.729
in the E-utilities book -- if you have any
questions, that's the place to look. We don't

00:04:51.729 --> 00:04:55.509
have time today to go into the detail about
they'll how these particular command lines

00:04:55.509 --> 00:05:00.870
were put together but I will show you what
they can do and how they can produce reports

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:01.810
from the BLAST XML.

00:05:01.810 --> 00:05:09.759
I will switch over. I'm going to pull up some
text files that I have.

00:05:09.759 --> 00:05:18.240
This text file is available on the FTP site.
I'm going to show you a blast result on the

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:23.300
web. I will show you how to download the XML
and then we will go to the command line and

00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:25.590
show you how produce reports from that.

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:32.349
Let me get this BLAST RID up. This is a saved
search that I made the other day. I will copy

00:05:32.349 --> 00:05:40.639
the URL and paste it into a web browser.

00:05:40.639 --> 00:05:44.710
This is the result you might see if you did
a Web blast. Many people do web BLAST searches

00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:51.620
with multiple sequences and in this case I
had many sequences that I took the from a

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:59.900
meta-genome from sheep, sheep gut. One thing
that is a little bit of a problem for some people

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:04.889
using web BLAST is that you have this multipart
output. So to see the results you need to

00:06:04.889 --> 00:06:10.009
click on this to get to a new page and some
people don't like that and they like to have

00:06:10.009 --> 00:06:16.530
the whole thing. What you can do is go here
to the download tab -- download section -- notice

00:06:16.530 --> 00:06:23.080
that you can download the XML and XML 2. That
is a handy thing to do. A lot of people like

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:28.250
to archive this stuff -- I've heard a lot
of reports of people trying to save the pairwise

00:06:28.250 --> 00:06:33.659
output or trying to print out. If you save something
like a structured output format you can go

00:06:33.659 --> 00:06:39.210
back to it and generate just about anything
you want from that particular kind of report.

00:06:39.210 --> 00:06:48.400
I've saved the XML and XML 2 and I will show
you that briefly here -- back on my text editor.

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:52.259
Here's a classic blast xml here -- you can
see what it looks like.

00:06:52.259 --> 00:06:59.110
You can see the structure here. Let me see
if I can make this a little bigger.

00:06:59.110 --> 00:07:03.780
Here you can see the iteration -- the thing
that we are going to cycle over to pull things

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:09.839
out -- you can see the hits here -- each hit
will have an HSP which represents in alignment

00:07:09.839 --> 00:07:14.560
-- I can get out of the output if I want to
-- I can pull out the query sequence and hit

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:20.800
sequence and I can reconstruct blast paiewise.
Or any kind of report that suits my fancy

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:22.229
from this output.

00:07:22.229 --> 00:07:27.960
If you looked at the XML 2 what you'd find
is that you have this XML include document

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:32.360
that basically gives you a way of organizing
this so you could pull this together to make

00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:39.250
one document. Otherwise what you get is an
individual document for each query sequence.

00:07:39.250 --> 00:07:44.210
Here is what one of those looks like. What's
good about the XML2 -- it's organized a little

00:07:44.210 --> 00:07:50.539
bit more rationally. Instead of "Iteration"
have "results" -- we have reserved "iteration"

00:07:50.539 --> 00:07:56.479
for iterative blast and you can also pull
out some interesting things from this, for

00:07:56.479 --> 00:08:03.759
example you can get the tax ID shown here.
And a scientific name shown here -- these

00:08:03.759 --> 00:08:07.699
are the things you might want to get in the
blast output.

00:08:07.699 --> 00:08:14.889
Let's take a minute and show you some simple
command lines you can do with Xtract to get

00:08:14.889 --> 00:08:16.949
things out of here.

00:08:16.949 --> 00:08:24.210
These are coming from that text document.
I have the webinar notes here, I've got the

00:08:24.210 --> 00:08:30.460
traditional BLAST XML and then in the subdirectory
I have the ones from XML 2. I will show you

00:08:30.460 --> 00:08:36.820
a couple of commands. I'll go back to the
notepad here -- my webinar notes -- basically

00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:43.370
what you do to run one of these Xtract scripts
is you have to take an input file -- what

00:08:43.370 --> 00:08:51.240
you can do is cat the file then pipe it into
xtract -- for example, the simple thing to

00:08:51.240 --> 00:09:01.300
do is to get the XML structure. If I take
this one and go ahead and pipe that into another

00:09:01.300 --> 00:09:09.100
command, more -- that's a quick way to see
how this is structured and then you can pick

00:09:09.100 --> 00:09:15.070
out -- you might want to go through the iteration
there to get your particular hits out.

00:09:15.070 --> 00:09:21.630
Likewise you can do the same thing for XML
2. I have a commandline that does that. In

00:09:21.630 --> 00:09:29.389
this case what I have to do is use the Unix
commandline XML tool to put those files together

00:09:29.389 --> 00:09:42.980
before I send them to xtract. I can copy that.
I can paste it here. Again I'll pipe it to

00:09:42.980 --> 00:09:43.980
more.

00:09:42.980 --> 00:09:49.639
So I can see the way the blast XML2 is structured
and I can focus on the results here to get

00:09:49.639 --> 00:09:52.620
out my particular hits that I want to.

00:09:52.620 --> 00:10:00.579
I'm show you one more. This is one where you
can take the XML to produce something equivalent

00:10:00.579 --> 00:10:06.670
to a tab-delimited output or something like
that. A simple thing to do -- I will focus

00:10:06.670 --> 00:10:13.720
on XML 2. One of the simplest things to do
is -- suppose you want to find out what the

00:10:13.720 --> 00:10:24.649
best hit is for every query that I used. I
can use this commandline here and this particular

00:10:24.649 --> 00:10:29.589
commandline argument for xtract called first,
which gives me the first of each kind of element

00:10:29.589 --> 00:10:30.970
and presents that.

00:10:30.970 --> 00:10:44.620
I will copy that one. Let's pipe that into
more. And unfortunately, I've got the font

00:10:44.620 --> 00:10:48.380
pumped up so it's a little bit awkward
to read this but if I made it smaller you

00:10:48.380 --> 00:10:51.380
couldn't see it very well. I hope you can
see this.

00:10:51.380 --> 00:10:57.050
Here I've basically got it on each line. I've
got the query ID, the subject ID. the title

00:10:57.050 --> 00:11:02.690
of the hit sequence. So on and so forth. That's
a handy thing to get out of there.

00:11:02.690 --> 00:11:08.100
A more complete set of things I could do if
I wanted -- actually I have the scientific

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:15.980
name on the last one there. If I go back here
to the second script -- the second command

00:11:15.980 --> 00:11:22.060
line -- will basically present all the hits
for each sequence but I can use another -- and

00:11:22.060 --> 00:11:28.550
it comes out as a long line -- what I can
do that's a little more useful -- it's this

00:11:28.550 --> 00:11:35.070
long commandline here that takes advantage
of the ability to store an element as a variable

00:11:35.070 --> 00:11:39.300
and Eric talked about this in his talk the
other week.

00:11:39.300 --> 00:11:43.399
Basically I can take the query ID, which is
in a different part of the XML and repeat

00:11:43.399 --> 00:11:50.220
that when I print my elements. So that comes
first and then I'll get the ID of the hit

00:11:50.220 --> 00:11:54.959
sequence, the title, the E value, and the
scientific name of the organism.

00:11:54.959 --> 00:12:04.570
So I'll go ahead and just put that one in
there and I'll go ahead and pipe that to more.

00:12:04.570 --> 00:12:10.630
Again, we have the problem of wrapping but I
think you can see what is happening here.

00:12:10.630 --> 00:12:17.259
Here is my query sequence. Here is my first
hit sequence and the query sequences change

00:12:17.259 --> 00:12:20.110
as we go down and we get different ones.

00:12:20.110 --> 00:12:25.290
I've got each hit, the query sequence, the
subject sequence, the title of the thing I

00:12:25.290 --> 00:12:32.339
hit, the E value here, then the scientific
name of the organism.

00:12:32.339 --> 00:12:39.779
That is plenty to show you today for a short
webinar. I'll stop talking now. We will leave

00:12:39.779 --> 00:12:45.990
the room open for about five minutes if anyone
has questions. If you haven't already asked,

00:12:45.990 --> 00:12:48.110
we can entertain those now.

00:12:48.110 --> 00:12:53.630
While you think about those, let me show you
a couple of things here.

00:12:53.630 --> 00:12:58.850
For more information, remember we have a webinars
and courses page, a YouTube channel -- this

00:12:58.850 --> 00:13:03.820
recording will be up on the YouTube channel.
We have a blog. In fact there's an article

00:13:03.820 --> 00:13:08.820
about using EDirect that's worth your time
to look at. We have email list announcements

00:13:08.820 --> 00:13:14.720
and since we talked about two different different
things -- EDirect and BLAST -- there is a BLAST

00:13:14.720 --> 00:13:18.509
announce and an E-Utilities announce email
alias that might be useful to subscribe

00:13:18.509 --> 00:13:22.050
to and that link shows you how to subscribe.

00:13:22.050 --> 00:13:30.579
As always if you have questions in general,
you can write to us at, info@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov.

00:13:30.579 --> 00:13:31.769
We don't have any questions?

00:13:31.769 --> 00:13:37.100
Okay, I will go ahead and wrap up the presentation.
If you think of anything afterwards, you can

00:13:37.100 --> 00:13:41.579
always write to us at webinars@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov.

00:13:41.579 --> 00:13:50.680
And we will do some others in the series of
using EDirect with BLAST. In future webinars

00:13:50.680 --> 00:13:55.100
we will talk about how to take the output
and pipe it back into EDirect to get objects

00:13:55.100 --> 00:14:01.230
down from Entrez, for example taxonomy information
or to get the sequences back from Entrez and

00:14:01.230 --> 00:14:05.370
things like that.
Thanks for attending. I will end the meeting

00:14:05.370 --> 00:14:06.110
now.

